      "title": "Conflict-Zone Civilian Documentation & Humanitarian Safety Guide",
      "focus": "Journalist Protection · Humanitarian Healthcare · Civilian Documentation · Conflict Trauma",
      "initiativeStatus": "Proposed Initiative",
      "deliverable": "A proposed educational framework connecting press freedom, humanitarian medical neutrality, civilian documentation, and trauma ethics for journalists, healthcare workers, and human rights researchers in conflict areas.\n\nProposed components:\n• Civilian Journalist Protection: Detail legal protections under Article 79 of Additional Protocol I of the Geneva Conventions, non-operational media safety planning, and secure reporting of threats.\n• Humanitarian Healthcare Worker Safety: Examine legal safeguards for medical personnel and facilities, trauma triage principles, and medical neutrality in active hostilities.\n• Civilian Documentation & Evidence Integrity: Provide best practices for collecting corroborated eyewitness testimony, preserving metadata and digital records, and protecting vulnerable sources.\n• Conflict-Related Public Health: Document the collateral impacts of warfare on civilian healthcare infrastructure, maternal care, infectious disease, and chronic disease management.\n• Frontline Wellbeing & Trauma-Informed Ethics: Address secondary traumatic stress, emotional decompression, and responsible interview methods that avoid re-traumatising survivors.\n\nUltimateHealth Editorial Perspective:\nShireen Abu Akleh's quarter-century of journalism demonstrated that recording the lived experiences of civilians living through conflict is a vital public-health and humanitarian imperative.\n\nHer voice brought clarity, dignity, and calm authority to stories that might otherwise have been silenced. A faithful tribute must honour her legacy by defending the civilian status of journalists, upholding rigorous evidence-based reporting, and demanding that international legal safeguards for media workers are uncompromisingly enforced."
