    "lastVerifiedAt": "2026-11-07",
    "coreMessage": "“In spite of everything, I still believe that people are really good at heart.” Education, empathy, and historical truth help societies recognise dehumanisation before prejudice becomes institutional persecution. Remembering the individual lives behind historical atrocities is essential to protecting human dignity.",
    "whoWasThisPerson": "Annelies Marie Frank, known to the world as Anne Frank, was born on 12 June 1929 in Frankfurt am Main, Germany, to a Jewish family.\n\nAs Nazi persecution intensified across Germany, her family emigrated to Amsterdam in 1933, where Anne grew up, attended school, learned Dutch, and developed a passionate interest in reading, history, and creative writing.\n\nShe was an observant, witty, and deeply introspective teenager who harbored serious literary ambitions and dreamed of becoming a published author and professional journalist.\n\nHer personal diary reveals not only the escalating shadow of state-sponsored antisemitic persecution, but also her blossoming inner identity, family relationships, adolescent tensions, intellectual yearnings, and enduring hopes for the future.\n\nToday, Anne Frank's voice endures as one of history's most intimate and poignant testimonies of youth, resilience, and moral conscience under total war and totalitarian oppression.",
    "contributions": "Anne began keeping her diary on her thirteenth birthday, 12 June 1942, receiving the red-and-white checkered notebook just weeks before persecution forced her family into hiding.\n\nOn 6 July 1942, the Frank family entered concealment in the rear annex building at Prinsengracht 263 in Amsterdam, eventually joined by four other Jewish residents in the space that became known as the Secret Annex.\n\nFor more than two years, Anne documented daily life in confinement under the constant dread of discovery, articulating the emotional hardships of isolation, family disagreements, and the agonizing uncertainty endured by European Jews under Nazi occupation.\n\nIn 1944, upon hearing a radio broadcast from the Dutch government-in-exile urging citizens to preserve wartime diaries and accounts, Anne methodically revised and rewrote her entries with the clear goal of publishing a book after the war.\n\nHer writing demonstrated remarkable psychological insight and moral clarity, giving universal expression to a young person's longing for freedom, justice, and the affirmation of human goodness despite the catastrophe unfolding around her.",
    "factualContext": "On the morning of 4 August 1944, German Security Service officers and Dutch collaborators raided the building at Prinsengracht 263, arresting the eight people hiding in the Secret Annex along with two non-Jewish helpers, Victor Kugler and Johannes Kleiman.\n\nThe circumstances of the discovery remain historically unresolved; although deliberate betrayal has long been suspected, archival records and Anne Frank House investigations confirm that no informant has ever been conclusively identified, and the raid may have originated from separate administrative or fraud investigations.\n\nFollowing detention in Amsterdam and Westerbork transit camp, the eight occupants were deported on the final transport to Auschwitz-Birkenau in September 1944, where Anne was separated from her father, Otto Frank, and subjected to forced labor.\n\nIn late autumn 1944, Anne and her sister Margot were transferred to the Bergen-Belsen concentration camp in northern Germany, a facility overwhelmed by severe overcrowding, starvation rations, freezing conditions, and catastrophic sanitation.\n\nBoth sisters contracted typhus amid an uncontrolled camp epidemic and died in Bergen-Belsen in 1945; although post-war Dutch authorities officially recorded their deaths as 31 March 1945, comprehensive 2015 research by the Anne Frank House concluded that they most likely succumbed in February 1945, though the exact day remains unrecorded.",
    "societalImpact": "Following the raid, helper Miep Gies retrieved and safely preserved Anne's scattered notebooks and papers in a desk drawer, intending to return them to Anne upon her hoped-for return.\n\nWhen Otto Frank returned to Amsterdam in 1945 as the sole survivor among the eight occupants of the Secret Annex, Miep delivered the preserved writings to him; deeply moved by his daughter's literary depth, Otto transcribed and prepared the manuscript for publication.\n\nThe diary was first published in the Netherlands in 1947 under the title 'Het Achterhuis' (The Secret Annex) and has since reached global readership through translations, stage adaptations, and educational curricula in more than 75 languages.\n\nAnne Frank's writings transformed global historical consciousness, demonstrating the catastrophic human consequences of antisemitism, racism, and totalitarian violence through the authentic voice of an adolescent writer.\n\nThe preserved building at Prinsengracht 263 was established as the Anne Frank House museum in 1960, functioning as an international educational center dedicated to countering prejudice, protecting human dignity, and promoting historical truth for future generations.",
    "actionableLesson": "The life and writing of Anne Frank demonstrate that history is shaped not merely by institutional records and military events, but by individual human lives, ordinary hopes, and voices that deserve to be heard. Societies must recognise dehumanisation before prejudice becomes institutionalised into state-sanctioned persecution.\n\nGovernments, educational systems, and civil society must uphold six vital imperatives: confront antisemitism and discrimination at their earliest signs; protect religious, ethnic, and minority communities with equal legal guarantees; preserve primary historical evidence, diaries, and survivor accounts against revisionism; teach empathy through verifiable facts and critical thinking; safeguard the dignity, safety, and creative expression of children everywhere; and defend historical truth against political denial and fabricated certainties.",
    "editorialNotes": "Historical and editorial qualifications: (1) Anne Frank was arrested on 4 August 1944 at Prinsengracht 263; the cause of discovery has never been conclusively established, and the Anne Frank House treats betrayal theories (including the 2022 cold-case hypothesis) as unproven. (2) Date of death: Officially recorded post-war as 31 March 1945, but 2015 Anne Frank House research concluded she and Margot most likely died of typhus in February 1945; the exact day remains unknown. (3) Starvation and camp brutality formed the catastrophic backdrop, with typhus as the documented cause of death. (4) Publication: More than 75 languages documented by Anne Frank House. Preserved by Miep Gies, prepared and published by Otto Frank. (5) Described as one of the world's most widely read personal accounts of the Holocaust rather than 'the most influential youth testimony'. (6) Categorised under 'Historical Persecution, Youth Voice & Human Dignity' within 'education-and-dignity'.",
